(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which description of the inflammatory phase of wound healing is accurate?","choices":["Days 1 to 3: type I collagen is laid down in basket-weave orientation","Days 1 to 3: a fibrin hemostatic plug forms, neutrophils and macrophages remove dead tissue, and growth factors and cytokines are secreted to signal the next phase","Days 4 to 21: granulation tissue forms, comprised of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells that rebuild the wound bed from below over three weeks","Days 21 to one year: type III collagen is replaced by type I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The phase both cleans the wound and issues the signal that starts proliferation. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which statement describes the source of anterior epistaxis?","choices":["The anterior ethmoidal artery, about 10 percent","Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall, about 90 percent","Kiesselbach's plexus on the anterior septum, about 90 percent","The sphenopalatine artery, about 50 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anterior epistaxis, 90 percent of cases, comes from Kiesselbach's plexus on the anterior septum; the posterior 10 percent come from Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the symptom set of acute angle-closure glaucoma?","choices":["Severe eye pain, headache, halos around lights, cloudy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, and nausea and vomiting","Painless gradual bilateral blurring with glare at night","Flashing lights, a sudden shower of floaters, and a curtain falling across the field","Gradual central distortion with a scotoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — iris bombé blocks aqueous outflow totally, and the rapid pressure spike above 50 mmHg produces pain, headache, halos, a cloudy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil and vomiting. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Ulcerative inflammation is characterized by which change?","choices":["Formation of a membrane-like covering over intact and otherwise viable mucosa","Damage to blood vessels occurring with other exudates","Coagulative necrosis of an entire limb segment","Necrosis and sloughing of surface epithelium, exposing underlying connective tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That exposure of the underlying connective tissue is what defines the ulcerative pattern. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Where does the gene damage in carcinogenesis come from?","choices":["It is always acquired during life and never inherited","It arises only from errors during deoxyribonucleic acid replication","Inherited, and/or acquired from chemicals, radiation or microbes","It is always inherited through the germline and never acquired during life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — damaged genes may be inherited or develop from chemical carcinogens, ultraviolet and ionizing radiation, and microbes: viruses and one bacterium. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the tissue basis of a papule?","choices":["Focal loss of the epidermis that does not penetrate below the dermal-epidermal junction beneath it","Hyperplasia of the epidermis with hyperkeratosis, plus inflammation and dense collagen fibers in the dermis","Fluid containing inflammatory cells collecting within or beneath the epidermis","Separation of epidermis from dermis with fluid accumulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A papule is an elevated solid lesion under 5 millimeters, built from epidermal hyperplasia with hyperkeratosis over dermal inflammation, as in seborrheic keratosis. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Digestion of foreign material by activated neutrophils and macrophages involves which four steps?","choices":["Vasodilation, exudation, stasis, and repair","Adhesion, transmigration, aggregation, and chemotaxis","Recognition, attachment, engulfment, and degradation","Opsonization, complement fixation, lysis, and clearance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those four, and recognition and attachment are enhanced when opsonins are present. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which features mark vertigo as PERIPHERAL rather than central?","choices":["Absence of nystagmus entirely","Gradual onset, vertical nystagmus, non-suppressible, with neurological deficits","Gradual onset with hearing loss and tinnitus","Sudden onset, horizontal or rotational nystagmus, fatigable, suppressed by visual fixation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral vertigo involves the inner ear or cranial nerve VIII, with sudden onset and prominent horizontal or rotational nystagmus that is fatigable and suppressed by visual fixation. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What effects does serotonin have?","choices":["Pain generation and leukocyte chemotaxis","Opsonization of biomaterials","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Formation of a fibrous network trapping microorganisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serotonin causes vasodilation and increased vascular permeability, the same pair of vascular effects as histamine. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A benign tumor arises from cartilage. Which category does it fall into?","choices":["Epithelial, since cartilage lines a surface","Glandular, so it would be called an adenoma","Neither, since the classification covers only epithelial tumors","Mesenchymal, since cartilage is supportive tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mesenchymal tumors arise from supportive tissue: connective tissue, adipose tissue, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do hypertrophic scars and keloids depart from normal healing?","choices":["Type I collagen is replaced by weaker type III in basket-weave orientation","Fibroblast dysregulation prolongs the proliferative phase, collagen deposition and degradation become imbalanced during remodeling, and collagen bundles develop haphazardly and exceed the boundaries of the initial wound","Keratinocyte division slows and elastin degrades, thinning the tissue","The inflammatory phase never resolves, so granulation tissue never forms at all and the wound remains open indefinitely without ever progressing to the proliferative or remodeling phases that normally follow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two phases go wrong, and exceeding the original wound margin is the defining outcome. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which description of collagen in the remodeling phase of wound healing is accurate?","choices":["Collagen production continues to increase indefinitely","Type III collagen is replaced with stronger type I collagen oriented in small parallel bundles, whereas normal dermis has a basket-weave orientation","Type I collagen is replaced with weaker type III collagen oriented in a basket-weave pattern throughout the scar tissue that results","Collagen is degraded entirely and replaced by elastin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In remodeling, from day 21 to one year, type III collagen gives way to stronger type I in small parallel bundles, unlike the basket-weave of normal dermis. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which lid deformity goes with dacryoadenitis?","choices":["An outward-turning lid margin","A drooping upper lid from levator weakness","An S-shaped lid deformity","An inward-turning lid margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — dacryoadenitis is inflammation or infection of the lacrimal gland, causing superotemporal orbital rim pain and an S-shaped lid deformity. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What makes the eye the only place in the body where live neural tissue can be seen directly?","choices":["The cornea contains myelinated tracts that can be seen on slit lamp","The sclera is thin enough to transilluminate the whole globe","The optic nerve head is visible through the pupil without cutting any tissue","The retina can be biopsied more safely than any other neural tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the eye is a nervous window: the only place in the body where live neural tissue and a cranial nerve, the optic nerve head, can be seen directly. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are the steps of hematogenous spread, in order?","choices":["Cells separate and degrade intercellular tissue with enzymes, invade the vessel, then travel","Tumor cells adhere more tightly to each other; the mass compresses a vessel; a single fragment travels to one other organ","Tumor cells enter lymphatic vessels at the margin and follow the natural route of drainage","Tumor invades through an organ surface into a body cavity and settles on the serosal membranes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — multiple tumor fragments travel to other organs, so one organ may end up with several metastatic nodules. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What can a hyperopic patient see well?","choices":["Objects far away","Objects up close","Neither near nor far without correction","Only objects directly in the central field"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hyperopia is farsightedness, so distance vision is the preserved one. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which description of the pathology of nail clubbing is accurate?","choices":["A halt of keratin production at the matrix","Incomplete keratinization at the nail bed","Increased capillary density, with increased release of vascular endothelial growth factor","Overgrowth of the connective tissue within the nail bed itself over a period of time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The main pathologic finding in clubbing is increased capillary density, with increased release of vascular endothelial growth factor. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What does falling differentiation mean for the behavior of a tumor?","choices":["Aggressiveness is unrelated to differentiation and depends only on stage","It becomes more aggressive only once the basement membrane has been breached","It becomes more aggressive","It becomes less aggressive, because poorly differentiated cells divide more slowly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — poorly differentiated cells stay in the proliferative pool, so the tumor grows faster; benign tumors are usually well differentiated and slow growing. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is episcleritis best characterized?","choices":["A vision-threatening emergency with severe deep boring pain and scleral melting","An infection of the corneal stroma","A granulomatous blockage of a meibomian gland","A self-limiting inflammatory blush, not vision-threatening and without severe pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — inflammation of the superficial episclera is a self-limiting blush with no severe pain and no threat to vision, unlike the deep, emergency inflammation of scleritis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What can an adenoma sometimes do that reflects its origin?","choices":["Produce finger-like projections from the epithelial surface","Secrete the hormone or hormones produced by its gland of origin","Invade through the basement membrane into surrounding tissue","Seed the peritoneal cavity through an organ surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the tumor retains a function of the tissue it came from. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is a malignant tumor of mesenchymal origin called?","choices":["An adenoma","A papilloma","A sarcoma","A carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — mesenchymal means supportive tissue, and the malignant form is the sarcoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which tumors are associated with an inherited alteration of the retinoblastoma protein?","choices":["Retinoblastoma, a rare childhood tumor of the eye, and osteosarcoma","Neurofibromatosis types 1 and 2, with tumors of the central and peripheral nervous system","Malignant melanoma","Familial adenomatous polyposis and colon cancer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same tumor suppressor, two very different tumors. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which description of actinic keratosis is accurate?","choices":["Benign proliferation of immature keratinocytes, carrying a higher risk for basal cell carcinoma of the head and neck region in later life especially","Human papillomavirus infection of basal cells, carrying higher risk for melanoma","Autoimmune T cell infiltration, carrying higher risk for lymphoma","Damage from excessive cumulative ultraviolet exposure leading to intraepidermal proliferation of dysplastic keratinocytes, carrying higher risk for squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratosis is the most common precancer, arising on skin damaged by chronic ultraviolet exposure, and it carries a higher risk of squamous cell carcinoma. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Why does stasis of blood flow matter mechanistically in acute inflammation?","choices":["It terminates the inflammatory response","It precedes leukocyte adhesion in the sequence, as slowed flow allows leukocytes to contact the endothelium","It marks the point at which acute inflammation gives way to chronic inflammation in the tissue","It reverses the earlier increase in permeability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemoconcentration and stasis sit immediately before leukocyte adhesion in the sequence of events of acute inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the pattern of onset in presbycusis?","choices":["Gradual, symmetrical and bilateral","Sudden and unilateral","Episodic and fluctuating","Gradual but strictly unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is sensorineural. Asymmetry would point away from aging toward a focal lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why does an ulcer scar while an erosion generally does not?","choices":["An ulcer involves a loss of melanocytes from the basal layer which cannot be reversed once it has occurred in the tissue at any point afterwards","An ulcer destroys collagen in the dermis, so healing must lay down new collagen; an erosion spares the dermal-epidermal junction and the dermis beneath it","An ulcer is infected and an erosion is sterile","An ulcer is larger, and size alone determines scarring"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Scarring is a dermal repair process, so a lesion confined above the junction has nothing to scar. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the three causes of chronic inflammation?","choices":["Trauma, thermal injury, and radiation exposure","Persistent infection, prolonged exposure to potentially toxic agents, and autoimmunity","Vasodilation, increased permeability, and exudation","Opsonization of foreign material, phagocytosis by neutrophils, and enzymatic degradation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Persistent infection, prolonged exposure to toxic agents and autoimmunity cause chronic inflammation; lupus is an autoimmune example. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which vascular change is directly responsible for allowing fluid and blood proteins to enter the interstitium?","choices":["Vasoconstriction of the draining venules","Endothelial contraction increasing capillary permeability","Hemoconcentration occurring within the capillary bed","Vasodilation of the supplying arterioles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endothelial contraction is what allows the increased permeability through which exudation occurs. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Besides invasion into adjacent structures, which abnormal interaction is a step in invasion?","choices":["Abnormal apoptosis of neighbouring stromal cells","Abnormal lymphatic valve function","Abnormal cell-substratum interaction","Abnormal cell-cycle checkpoint control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the cell's relationship with its own supporting matrix changes. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which description defines vertigo as distinct from dizziness?","choices":["A hallucination of motion, with NO syncope","A sensation of impending faint from cerebral hypoperfusion","Any unsteadiness on standing","Loss of consciousness preceded by spinning"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetry between the two sides is the mechanism, and the absence of syncope is the separator. Dizziness or lightheadedness is a non-vestibular sensation of impending faint. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What makes the middle ear vulnerable to the pressure changes behind otitis media?","choices":["It is an air-filled space vented only by the Eustachian tube","It has no blood supply","It is lined by keratinized skin","It is fluid-filled and cannot compress"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A sealed air pocket with one vent is why closing the vent drops the pressure and draws in fluid. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which chemical carcinogens are classically emphasized in occupational bladder cancer?","choices":["Aromatic amines","Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons","Alkylating agents","Asbestos fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — aromatic amines are classically emphasized in occupational bladder cancer, whereas tobacco supplies polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What tissue makes up a scale?","choices":["Dried sebum, cellular debris, blood or necrotic skin","Thickened epidermis induced by long-term scratching","Focal loss of epidermis and dermis with destruction of the collagen beneath","A compact portion of desquamating stratum corneum, varying in size and thickness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A scale is a compact portion of desquamating stratum corneum that varies in size and thickness, and psoriasis is an example. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Above what cup-to-disc ratio is optic disc cupping progressive?","choices":["Greater than 0.8","Greater than 1.0","Greater than 0.5","Greater than 0.2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — raised pressure compresses retinal ganglion cell axons, the cells die by apoptosis, and the cup widens to a cup-to-disc ratio above 0.5. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of wet, neovascular macular degeneration?","choices":["Extracellular lipofuscin debris accumulating beneath the retinal pigment epithelium","Fibrovascular membranes on the retinal surface exert traction","Ganglion cell axons undergo apoptosis from raised pressure","Hypoxia and inflammation trigger choroidal neovascularization, and new vessels breach beneath the pigment epithelium into the subretinal space"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — hypoxia and inflammation trigger choroidal neovascularization, and the new vessels breach beneath the pigment epithelium into the subretinal space, where they leak blood and serous fluid. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which skin cancer is the most common malignancy in humans?","choices":["Actinic keratosis, which is by far the most common malignancy occurring in human beings","Basal cell carcinoma, slow growing and rarely metastasizing, typically on the head and neck","Squamous cell carcinoma, rapidly metastasizing","Melanoma, slow growing and rarely metastasizing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin cancer and most common human malignancy; it grows slowly and rarely metastasizes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What single feature separates a pterygium from a pinguecula?","choices":["The pterygium encroaches onto the cornea; the pinguecula does not","The pterygium is yellow; the pinguecula is triangular","The pterygium is painful; the pinguecula is painless","The pterygium is bilateral; the pinguecula is unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both are fibrovascular proliferations from chronic ultraviolet, wind and dust exposure, but only the pterygium encroaches onto the cornea and can impair vision. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does a lesion of cranial nerve VIII produce the same audiometric pattern as cochlear damage?","choices":["Both add mass to the tympanic membrane","Both stiffen the ossicles","Both obstruct the canal","Both sit beyond the conducting chain, so both give sensorineural loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The category is defined by where the defect sits, not by which of the two structures failed. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How does human papillomavirus produce a common wart?","choices":["It triggers mast cell degranulation in the dermis","It digests keratin using keratinases and proteases","It invades epidermal basal cells through microabrasions, leading to proliferation of the epidermis","It infects the melanocytes that lie along the dermal-epidermal junction within the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Human papillomavirus invades epidermal basal cells through microabrasions, and that infection leads to proliferation of the epidermis. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How does dry macular degeneration affect vision?","choices":["Rapid central vision loss over days","Loss of peripheral vision first, with the central field spared until late","Sudden painless monocular blindness","Slow gradual loss of central detail, with distortion and a scotoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — slow degeneration of photoreceptors, pigment epithelium and choroid causes gradual loss of central detail, with metamorphopsia and scotoma. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How do the causes of anterior and posterior epistaxis differ?","choices":["Anterior from hypertension and anticoagulation; posterior from digital trauma","Both are caused predominantly by anticoagulation","Anterior from coagulopathy; posterior from low humidity","Anterior from local trauma and dry air; posterior from hypertension and anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anterior bleeds are local and mechanical; posterior bleeds reflect a systemic vascular or clotting problem, which is also why they are more profuse and carry airway risk. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes androgen regulation of hair?","choices":["Lanugo hair is androgen-regulated; terminal hair is not","All three types are equally androgen-dependent","Terminal hair is androgen-regulated; vellus hair grows independently of androgens","Vellus hair is regulated by androgens; terminal hair grows independently of them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thick terminal hair on the head, beard, axillae and pubic areas is androgen-regulated; fine vellus hair grows independently of androgens. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the natural course of a subconjunctival hemorrhage?","choices":["Benign, resolving over one to two weeks with bruise-like color changes","Progressive, threatening vision within days","Permanent, leaving a fibrovascular scar","Recurrent by definition, so it requires long-term ophthalmology follow-up"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — blood leaked from ruptured episcleral or conjunctival capillaries lies beneath the conjunctiva and clears in one to two weeks, changing color like a bruise. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which way does the nystagmus of labyrinthitis beat?","choices":["Vertical and non-suppressible","Absent, because hearing rather than balance is affected","Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating AWAY from the affected side","Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating TOWARD the affected side"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis gives horizontal-rotary nystagmus with the fast phase beating away from the affected side, with severe postural instability, gait ataxia and nausea. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does presbycusis make voices hard to discriminate in noisy environments?","choices":["High frequencies go first, taking the speech consonants","Low frequencies go first, taking the vowels","All frequencies fall equally","Only frequencies above the speech range are affected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Presbycusis begins with high-frequency tones, which include speech consonants such as /s/, /f/ and /t/, making voice discrimination difficult in noisy environments. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which pairing of share of circulating serum proteins and action is correct for complement components?","choices":["25%; responsible for the formation of a clot at the site of the injury or lesion","10%; chemotactic to neutrophils and monocytes, with the cascade able to damage bacteria","50%; primarily responsible for oncotic pressure","1%; acting solely as opsonins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Complement components make up 10% of circulating serum proteins, are chemotactic to neutrophils and monocytes, and can damage bacteria. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is canalithiasis?","choices":["Dislodged otoconia floating free in the semicircular canals","Accumulation of endolymph in the membranous labyrinth","Inflammatory debris within the cochlea after infection","Calcification of the stapes footplate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Canalithiasis means dislodged otoconia, the crystals, lying in the semicircular canals; the result is episodic vertigo, usually under a minute, with head position changes and no hearing loss. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which description fits the rods of the retina?","choices":["About 120 million, for dim light and peripheral vision","About 6 million, for dim light and peripheral vision","About 120 million, for color vision and sharp acuity","About 20 million, for color vision in bright light"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — about 120 million rods give high sensitivity for dim light and night vision, and they are found in the peripheral retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How do phagocytic cells recognize immunoglobulin G bound to foreign material?","choices":["Through histamine receptors on the endothelium","Through Fc receptors that recognize the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G","Through the complement receptors that bind to the C3b fragment","Through T cell receptors on the phagocyte surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages and neutrophils carry Fc receptors that bind the Fc portion of the antibody. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which pattern of inflammation is defined by mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane?","choices":["Catarrhal","Pseudomembranous","Serous","Hemorrhagic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Catarrhal inflammation is mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What distinguishes a vesicle from a bulla?","choices":["A vesicle is solid and a bulla is fluid-filled","A vesicle contains purulent material and a bulla contains serous fluid","A vesicle is up to 5 millimeters with fluid containing inflammatory cells collecting within or directly beneath the epidermis; a bulla is greater than 5 millimeters and involves separation of epidermis from dermis","A vesicle is greater than 5 millimeters and a bulla is up to 5 millimeters in diameter, and the two share an identical mechanism of fluid accumulation entirely within the epidermis rather than beneath it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The bulla represents a deeper plane of cleavage, not merely a bigger blister. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which pairing of patient group and organism carries particular risk in keratitis?","choices":["Contact lens wearers, from Staphylococcus aureus","Elderly patients, from Streptococcus pneumoniae","Immunosuppressed patients, from Candida","Contact lens wearers, from Pseudomonas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — contact lens wearers carry a Pseudomonas risk, and keratitis is an emergency because it can progress to stromal ulceration, perforation and endophthalmitis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which combination of effects does bradykinin produce?","choices":["Increased capillary permeability, pain, and possibly increased leukocyte chemotaxis","Vasoconstriction, reduced capillary permeability, and analgesia at the site","Antibody production and antigen presentation","Coagulation and trapping of exudate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bradykinin is the plasma-protein mediator that adds pain to the permeability effect. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which pairing gives an example of dysplasia with its usual cause?","choices":["Barrett's esophagus, usually caused by chronic acid reflux","Endometrial hyperplasia, usually caused by excess estrogen","Skeletal muscle hypertrophy, usually caused by resistance training","Cervical dysplasia, usually caused by human papillomavirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — high-risk human papillomavirus infects cervical epithelial cells, and the resulting uncontrolled proliferation raises the risk of progression from dysplasia to carcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which description matches the serous pattern of inflammation?","choices":["Mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane surface","Largely plasma with low protein content, occurring early or with mild inflammation","Large amounts of fibrinogen forming a thick meshwork","Remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serous inflammation is largely plasma, low in protein, and occurs early or when inflammation is mild. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Where do the pressure waves that begin hearing actually travel first?","choices":["The cochlear duct itself","The scala vestibuli","The semicircular canals","The scala tympani"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stapes movement at the oval window starts them there, and they displace endolymph in the cochlear duct. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which two malignancies is Helicobacter pylori associated with?","choices":["Hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and Burkitt lymphoma","Cervical carcinoma and oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma","Gastric adenocarcinoma and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — chronic Helicobacter pylori gastritis is associated with gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) lymphoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Why does the vertigo of Meniere disease come in attacks rather than continuously?","choices":["Inflammation resolves and recurs","Pressure builds until micro-ruptures occur, then re-equilibrates","The virus replicates in waves","Otoconia settle between episodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Defective resorption lets endolymph accumulate and balloon the scala media until micro-ruptures occur; that build-up and release makes it episodic. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why is the name agranulocyte somewhat misleading?","choices":["Because they are actually a subtype of polymorphonuclear leukocyte","Because these cells acquire granules only after activation","Because their granules are visible only in chronic inflammation","Because these cells do contain non-specific azurophilic granules — lysosomes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The name implies an absence of granules, but non-specific azurophilic granules (lysosomes) are present. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes tumor suppressor genes?","choices":["They promote regulated cell growth; the examples are growth factors and their receptors","They repair damaged deoxyribonucleic acid; the examples are BRCA-1 and BRCA-2","They trigger apoptosis in damaged cells; no specific examples are named","They inhibit cell growth; the examples are NF-1, NF-2, retinoblastoma and adenomatous polyposis coli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — losing them removes a brake rather than adding an accelerator. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
